#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""NCERT Class 3 Mathematics - Chapter 11 Part 1"""

slides_part1 = [{'id': 1, 'title': 'Juice Glass Capacity', 'subtitle': 'Discovering liquid volume equivalence between smaller and larger drinking glasses', 'speech': 'Welcome to our mathematics laboratory where we explore how liquids are measured and contained. Imagine visiting  a busy roadside fresh juice stall during a warm summer afternoon. The stall owner announces an exciting challenge:  drink six full glasses of refreshing juice to win a special party prize. However, customers immediately notice  something very important about the glassware arranged on the counter. The stall displays small narrow glasses as  well as large tall tumblers. Drinking six tiny glasses might sound like an immense quantity, but each small glass holds  only a modest amount of liquid. In contrast, drinking just two or three large tumblers could actually hold the exact same  total volume of juice. This everyday scenario introduces the foundational mathematical concept of capacity. Capacity  refers to the total amount of liquid that a container can hold when filled completely to its brim. When evaluating  liquid quantities, simply counting the number of glasses is not enough. We must always consider the physical size  and internal holding space of each individual glass. Recognizing that fewer large glasses can equal many small glasses  helps learners understand volume conservation, laying the groundwork for comparing capacities with confidence and clarity. This makes liquid measurement intuitive and engaging.', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'If 1 large tumbler holds the same amount of juice as 2 small glasses, how many small glasses equal 3 large tumblers?', 'options': ['6 small glasses (3 × 2)', '3 small glasses', '5 small glasses', '12 small glasses'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Multiply the number of large tumblers by 2.', 'explanation': 'Since 1 large tumbler = 2 small glasses, 3 large tumblers = 3 × 2 = 6 small glasses.'}, {'question': "What does the mathematical term 'capacity' mean?", 'options': ['The maximum amount of liquid a container can hold when filled to the brim', 'The height of the table where glasses rest', 'The color of the juice inside the glass', 'The price charged by the stall owner'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Think about internal volume and holding space.', 'explanation': 'Capacity is the maximum volume of liquid that a vessel or container can hold.'}]}, {'id': 2, 'title': 'Equal Container Pouring', 'subtitle': 'Pouring different glasses into identical transparent jars to compare liquid levels', 'speech': 'A fascinating visual puzzle occurs when people drink milk or water from glasses of different shapes. Suppose three  friends drink milk from three uniquely shaped glasses: one glass is tall and slender, the second is short and wide,  and the third has a fluted bowl shape. Looking at the filled glasses on the kitchen counter, it is very difficult to tell  who has the most milk. The tall glass looks deceptively full because the liquid level reaches high up, while the wide  glass looks less full even though its base is broad. How can we resolve this debate fairly and scientifically? The  clever solution is an equalizing pouring activity. We take three identical, transparent cylindrical jars and pour the  contents of each glass into its own jar. Because all three test jars have the exact same width and shape, comparing  the resulting liquid heights gives an immediate, truthful answer. The jar with the highest liquid level contained the  greatest volume of milk, while the jar with the lowest level held the least. This simple pouring test demonstrates that  container shape can easily trick our eyes, but standardizing the vessel reveals true volume instantly. Honest comparisons require uniform reference conditions across all containers.', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'Why does pouring liquids into identical transparent jars allow a fair comparison of volume?', 'options': ['Because identical jars have equal width, so higher liquid level directly means more volume', 'Because pouring liquid makes it magically multiply', 'Because transparent glass makes milk turn sweeter', 'Because short glasses always hold zero milk'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'When vessel width is constant, liquid height accurately reflects total volume.', 'explanation': 'Standardizing the container shape eliminates visual illusions, allowing height to directly indicate comparative volume.'}, {'question': 'Why might a tall slender glass trick someone into thinking it holds more than a short wide mug?', 'options': ['Its high liquid level creates a visual illusion of greater capacity', 'Tall glasses are always heavier than wide mugs', 'Slender glasses make liquid freeze faster', 'Wide mugs are invisible from above'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Our eyes often focus only on vertical height while ignoring container width.', 'explanation': 'People often mistakenly judge volume by liquid height alone, overlooking that a wide base can hold substantially more liquid.'}]}, {'id': 3, 'title': 'Vessel Filling Estimates', 'subtitle': 'Estimating and verifying container capacities using small bowls and drinking glasses', 'speech': 'To build strong intuitive number sense for liquid quantities, learners participate in hands-on vessel filling activities.  In this investigation, students collect a variety of containers from around the classroom and home, such as small soup  bowls, drinking glasses, water bottles, and plastic jugs. Before pouring a single drop of water, students make an educated  guess. For instance, how many small bowls of water will it take to fill one drinking glass completely? A student might  predict three bowls. Next, they test their prediction by filling the small bowl to its brim and carefully pouring it  into the glass, counting each bowl aloud until the glass is full. Repeating this process with larger vessels reveals  exciting ratios: it might take four full glasses of water to fill a one-litre water bottle, and five bottles to fill a large  washing bucket. Guessing first and checking afterward trains children to visualize volume, calibrate their mental estimates,  and understand informal unit multiplication. Every vessel becomes both a container and a non-standard measuring tool, showing  how smaller units repeatedly combine to fill larger spaces without spilling precious resources. Hands-on pouring practice connects tactile motor exploration directly with early foundational volume estimation habits for young growing students.', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'If it takes exactly 4 glasses of water to fill 1 water bottle, how many glasses fill 2 identical bottles?', 'options': ['8 glasses (4 × 2)', '4 glasses', '6 glasses', '2 glasses'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Multiply the number of glasses per bottle by 2 bottles.', 'explanation': 'Since 1 bottle requires 4 glasses, 2 bottles require 4 × 2 = 8 glasses of water.'}, {'question': 'What is the primary benefit of guessing before verifying liquid capacity with actual pouring?', 'options': ['It develops mental visualization and improves estimation accuracy over time', 'It guarantees that water will never spill on the floor', 'It makes the drinking glass grow larger', 'It changes water into sweet lemonade'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Predicting and checking sharpens intuitive spatial judgment.', 'explanation': 'Making predictions before testing actively calibrates mental estimation skills and deepens intuitive volume comprehension.'}]}, {'id': 4, 'title': 'Lemonade Utensil Measures', 'subtitle': 'Analyzing real-world serving utensils and solving capacity word problems at market stalls', 'speech': 'At a vibrant school fair, running a refreshing lemonade stall offers a wonderful opportunity to practice capacity  word problems. The stall counter features diverse utensils: deep metal ladles, mixing bowls, serving glasses, and large  water jugs. To prepare tasty lemonade efficiently, the stall attendants must understand how these utensils relate to one  another mathematically. By testing their equipment, the learners discover that one large jug holds enough lemonade to fill  exactly ten customer glasses. Furthermore, when serving from a bowl, it takes three full ladles of lemonade to fill a single  glass. If a thirsty group of visitors orders five glasses of lemonade, how many ladles must be scooped from the bowl?  Since each glass requires three ladles, five glasses require five times three, which equals fifteen ladles in total. Similarly,  if two large jugs are prepared for the afternoon rush, the stall can serve two times ten, or twenty happy customers.  Working through these practical scenarios connects basic arithmetic operations of multiplication and division directly to  everyday hospitality, helping students see how capacity calculations keep community events running smoothly and fairly. Practical community experiences prove that mathematical arithmetic plays a vital role in everyday cooperative social gatherings and festive events.', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'If 1 glass of lemonade requires 3 ladles, how many ladles are needed to fill 4 glasses?', 'options': ['12 ladles (4 × 3)', '7 ladles (4 + 3)', '10 ladles', '1 ladle'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Multiply the number of glasses by 3 ladles each.', 'explanation': '4 glasses × 3 ladles per glass = 12 ladles in total.'}, {'question': 'A lemonade jug fills exactly 10 glasses. How many full jugs are needed to serve 30 customers?', 'options': ['3 jugs (30 ÷ 10)', '2 jugs', '5 jugs', '10 jugs'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Divide the total number of customers by 10 glasses per jug.', 'explanation': '30 customers ÷ 10 glasses per jug = 3 full jugs of lemonade.'}]}, {'id': 5, 'title': 'Standard Litre Benchmark', 'subtitle': 'Introducing the international standard litre unit for dependable liquid measurement', 'speech': 'While bowls, ladles, and glasses are convenient for informal kitchen cooking, using them for commercial trade leads to  endless disagreements. A glass in one household might hold twice as much liquid as a glass in a neighbouring home. To  ensure fair and accurate transactions everywhere, societies established a universally recognized standard unit for measuring  liquid capacity: the litre. The litre is abbreviated in mathematical notation with the capital letter L or small letter l.  Every morning in neighbourhoods across India, milk vendors carry cylindrical metal measuring cans crafted to hold exactly  one litre of fresh milk. Packaged drinking water, cooking oil, and cartons of fruit juice are also sold in standard one-litre  containers. When you hold a full one-litre bottle of water, you can feel its physical weight and observe its exact liquid  volume. Whether milk is measured in a village dairy or water is bottled in a metropolitan factory, one litre represents an  identical, unvarying quantity of liquid. Introducing the standard litre provides students with a reliable mathematical anchor  for understanding volume across the globe with precision. Universal standards unite people everywhere under a shared, trusted mathematical framework of reliable, honest physical measurement across all cities, towns, and neighbouring villages.', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'What is the standard metric base unit used internationally to measure liquid capacity?', 'options': ['The litre (L)', 'The handspan', 'The metre rod', 'The kilogram'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Look for the liquid unit commonly printed on drinking water bottles.', 'explanation': 'The litre (symbol L or l) is the universal metric standard unit for measuring liquid volume and capacity.'}, {'question': 'Why do milkmen and commercial vendors use standardized metal litre measuring cans?', 'options': ['To guarantee that every customer receives an identical, fair, and accurate quantity', 'Because metal cans make the milk change colour', 'To make the milk evaporate into thin air', 'Because standard cans are impossible to wash'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Standard tools guarantee uniform fairness in trade.', 'explanation': 'Standardized litre measures ensure that quantities remain consistent, trustworthy, and completely fair for all buyers and sellers.'}]}, {'id': 6, 'title': 'Comparing One Litre', 'subtitle': 'Classifying familiar household containers into capacities less than, equal to, or greater than one litre', 'speech': 'Once learners understand the physical scale of a standard one-litre bottle, they can investigate and classify familiar  household containers into three distinct capacity groups: containers holding less than one litre, containers holding  approximately one litre, and containers holding more than one litre. Small personal utensils like a teacup, a soup spoon,  a medicine dropper, a baby feeding bottle, or a school drinking glass all hold significantly less liquid than a one-litre  bottle, placing them firmly in the less than one litre group. Next, examine containers designed around the single-litre  benchmark: a standard personal thermal flask, a carton of soy milk, or a reusable cycling water bottle typically hold  about one litre. Finally, look at large utility vessels around the home and garden. A kitchen pressure cooker, a bathroom  bathing bucket, a watering can, a plastic water tank, or an overhead storage reservoir hold vastly more than one litre.  Sorting containers into these three categories trains young mathematicians to evaluate volume quickly in everyday life,  fostering intuitive estimation skills that prove invaluable during cooking, cleaning, and practical chores. Classifying everyday vessels by capacity transforms abstract volume numbers into tangible, memorable real-world understanding for all young learners discovering geometry in their daily lives.', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'Which of the following everyday vessels holds significantly LESS than 1 litre of liquid?', 'options': ['An ordinary porcelain teacup', 'A bathroom bathing bucket', 'A garden watering can', 'An overhead water tank'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Think about which vessel is used for taking small sips of warm tea.', 'explanation': 'A teacup typically holds about 150 to 200 millilitres, which is much less than 1 litre (1,000 mL).'}, {'question': 'Which household container is designed to hold substantially MORE than 1 litre of water?', 'options': ['A large bathroom bathing bucket', 'A small soup spoon', 'A medicine dropper bottle', 'An ink cartridge'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Think of a heavy container used for bathing or mopping floors.', 'explanation': 'A standard bathing bucket holds approximately 15 to 20 litres, which is far greater than 1 litre.'}]}, {'id': 7, 'title': 'Half Litre Portions', 'subtitle': 'Discovering that two half-litre portions combine perfectly to make one complete litre', 'speech': 'While a full litre is a wonderful standard benchmark, many beverages and liquid items are packaged in smaller quantities.  This leads us to explore fractional parts of a litre, beginning with the half-litre. If you take a full one-litre bottle  of water and pour it equally between two identical mugs until both mugs reach the exact same liquid height, each mug holds  one-half of a litre. In mathematical terms, this is written as one over two litres, or one-half litre. In the metric system,  one full litre contains one thousand millilitres. Therefore, dividing one thousand by two equal parts gives exactly five  hundred millilitres for each half-litre. Many dairy shops sell milk in convenient half-litre pouches. If you buy two  half-litre pouches of milk, combining their contents gives five hundred millilitres plus five hundred millilitres, which  equals one thousand millilitres or one whole litre. Understanding that two half-litres make one whole litre helps children  grasp the arithmetic of fractions through tangible liquid pouring, linking fractional symbols directly to practical kitchen measurements. Seeing how two equal portions combine to restore a single whole unit grounds abstract fraction symbols in concrete, physical liquid reality, making kitchen tasks and mathematical problem solving completely effortless.', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'How many half-litre mugs of water are needed to completely fill a 1-litre bottle?', 'options': ['Exactly 2 mugs', '4 mugs', '1 mug', '10 mugs'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Two halves always combine to form one whole.', 'explanation': '1/2 litre + 1/2 litre = 1 whole litre, so exactly 2 half-litre mugs fill a 1-litre bottle.'}, {'question': 'If a dairy sells milk in 500 mL pouches, what fraction of a litre is 1 pouch?', 'options': ['1/2 litre', '1/4 litre', '3/4 litre', '2 whole litres'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': '500 is half of 1,000.', 'explanation': 'Since 1 litre = 1,000 mL, 500 mL is exactly one-half of a litre (1/2 L).'}]}, {'id': 8, 'title': 'Quarter Litre Glasses', 'subtitle': 'Partitioning one litre into four equal quarter-litre glasses of two hundred fifty millilitres', 'speech': 'Just as we divided a litre into two equal halves, we can divide it even further into four equal parts called quarters.  Suppose we take a full one-litre jug of sweet mango juice and pour it equally into four identical drinking glasses. When  the jug is completely empty, each of the four glasses holds exactly one-quarter of a litre, written as one over four litres.  Since one full litre equals one thousand millilitres, dividing one thousand by four gives two hundred and fifty millilitres  for each glass. This means one-quarter of a litre is precisely two hundred and fifty millilitres. Notice the beautiful  fractional patterns that emerge: one glass is one-quarter litre, two glasses make two-quarters or one-half litre, three  glasses make three-quarters of a litre or seven hundred fifty millilitres, and four glasses together restore the complete  one-litre total. Having mental benchmarks for quarter, half, and three-quarter litres allows learners to solve kitchen recipes  and beverage sharing problems effortlessly, turning abstract fractions into friendly, thirst-quenching classroom discoveries. Exploring quarter partitions gives young learners strong proportional reasoning foundations, preparing them for advanced measurements in future science experiments, cooking recipes, and collaborative classroom projects that require careful, accurate volumetric calculations.', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'How many quarter-litre glasses of juice are required to fill a 1-litre jug?', 'options': ['4 glasses', '2 glasses', '8 glasses', '1 glass'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Four quarters make one whole.', 'explanation': 'Four 1/4-litre glasses combine to make 1 whole litre (4 × 1/4 = 1).'}, {'question': 'If 1 quarter-litre equals 250 mL, how many millilitres are in three-quarters of a litre (3/4 L)?', 'options': ['750 mL (3 × 250)', '500 mL', '1,000 mL', '250 mL'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Multiply 250 mL by 3 quarters.', 'explanation': 'Three quarters of a litre = 3 × 250 mL = 750 mL.'}]}]

